1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,170
Nintendo Switch Sports shows a win
banner and a celebration after a match.

2
00:00:07,480 --> 00:00:11,850
Ring Fit Adventure accompanies exercise
movements with evaluation feedback.

3
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:16,310
The same score can feel different
when a game acknowledges what you did.

4
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:20,710
We can distinguish calculating success from
expressing recognition of that success.

5
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:26,050
Today we examine how to recognize an
action, rather than how to add rewards.

6
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:30,745
Our original defense test applies the same
inputs and collision rules to both screens.

7
00:00:30,760 --> 00:00:34,330
We change the timing, wording, strength, and position of the praise.

8
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:39,150
Enjoyment needs human testing, while
logs can verify exactly what changed.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,960
Watch the ball roll and the pins fall in
Nintendo Switch Sports bowling.

10
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:47,140
The change in the pins is an outcome.

11
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:50,280
We do not add a Strike label absent from this
excerpt.

12
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:53,200
This is a starting point for separating an
observable

13
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:55,300
result from feeling that success was
acknowledged.

14
00:00:55,600 --> 00:01:00,420
In Hi-Fi RUSH, hit count, score and grading
displays change after attacks.

15
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:04,860
Distinguish the increasing numbers from the
brief Perfect response.

16
00:01:05,260 --> 00:01:07,700
Even on one screen, a record display and
acknowledgement

17
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:09,900
of a particular action serve different
purposes.

18
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,760
Ring Fit movement grades and a Switch Sports

19
00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:15,300
victory screen refer to different parts of
play.

20
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:20,380
First identify whether the response marks one
movement or the result of an entire match.

21
00:01:20,780 --> 00:01:23,600
As in our earlier comparison, preserving
reward rules while

22
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:26,260
choosing what success to reveal makes the
purpose clearer.

23
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:29,530
First, compare when the praise appears.

24
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:33,070
Both characters block the same attack at the same moment.

25
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:36,930
One side responds promptly; the other responds much later.

26
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:41,610
If the next attack arrives during the wait, it
is harder to connect praise with the action.

27
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:45,390
Try attaching a brief response close
to the successful collision result.

28
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:49,890
The signal can acknowledge the recent
action without a large display.

29
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:53,105
This does not prescribe one delay for every game.

30
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:57,930
It is a reason to check overlap with
the next action at normal speed.

31
00:01:58,733 --> 00:02:03,433
Watch the sequence of character movement and
changing displays during Hi-Fi RUSH attacks.

32
00:02:03,693 --> 00:02:07,453
We need to identify which attack a success
response follows.

33
00:02:07,733 --> 00:02:11,113
The beat indicator in the next excerpt guides
a future movement;

34
00:02:11,433 --> 00:02:12,953
it alone does not mean success.

35
00:02:13,113 --> 00:02:15,333
Separate the guidance from performance
feedback

36
00:02:15,333 --> 00:02:18,453
and observe when an actual attack result
appears.

37
00:02:18,933 --> 00:02:21,393
Whether there is time to read the last result
during

38
00:02:21,393 --> 00:02:24,313
continued play connects to our delay
comparison.

39
00:02:24,313 --> 00:02:29,653
We are observing movement and visible feedback
at normal speed, with the source music muted.

40
00:02:30,253 --> 00:02:32,513
Ring Fit guidance to hold a pose and the
following

41
00:02:32,513 --> 00:02:35,053
grade also appear at different moments.

42
00:02:35,533 --> 00:02:39,753
Hold and Return are movement instructions;
Great and Good are performance feedback.

43
00:02:40,233 --> 00:02:42,893
Do not treat guidance and praise as the same
event

44
00:02:42,893 --> 00:02:44,813
merely because text appears in a similar
place.

45
00:02:45,617 --> 00:02:49,653
A generic compliment may not explain what went well.

46
00:02:49,863 --> 00:02:53,873
This test blocks an attack, then moves upward to dodge the next one.

47
00:02:54,083 --> 00:02:58,993
One screen repeats the same praise; the
other distinguishes blocking from dodging.

48
00:02:59,143 --> 00:03:02,413
A brief label names the action actually
verified by the collision rules.

49
00:03:02,683 --> 00:03:07,433
Calling a button press a perfect block can misrepresent the result.

50
00:03:07,683 --> 00:03:11,373
Choose the label from the successful
outcome, rather than the input event alone.

51
00:03:11,743 --> 00:03:15,413
That information can help the player
identify an action to try again.

52
00:03:15,603 --> 00:03:20,113
Praise need not become an instruction
manual, but it need not hide its reason.

53
00:03:20,817 --> 00:03:26,217
Hi-Fi RUSH combo attacks show both input
guidance and actual hit results.

54
00:03:26,657 --> 00:03:30,977
Follow the button instructions and
acknowledgement of the attack separately.

55
00:03:31,497 --> 00:03:35,777
The existence of an input does not prove that
the movement succeeded.

56
00:03:36,217 --> 00:03:42,077
When designing praise, first name the action:
blocking, dodging or attack timing.

57
00:03:42,257 --> 00:03:46,317
Ring Fit instructions to hold or slowly lower
guide a pose.

58
00:03:46,577 --> 00:03:50,617
Observe how their purpose differs from the
subsequent Great grade.

59
00:03:50,817 --> 00:03:55,657
Racket guidance in Switch Sports also tells us
which direction to move.

60
00:03:55,857 --> 00:03:59,477
Combat feedback follows a performed action;

61
00:03:59,797 --> 00:04:01,937
look at the connected event, beyond the
wording.

62
00:04:02,317 --> 00:04:07,597
Rewriting Good job to name the action can
reveal what to change in your own game.

63
00:04:08,350 --> 00:04:12,587
Using the largest celebration for every
success removes a distinction between moments.

64
00:04:12,777 --> 00:04:17,867
A match win in Switch Sports illustrates
a broader celebration at a stopping point.

65
00:04:18,137 --> 00:04:22,787
The same scale after every small
success can cover the next situation.

66
00:04:22,897 --> 00:04:26,167
Our test uses a small marker for a single block.

67
00:04:26,297 --> 00:04:30,042
A stronger response appears only when a
sequence of successful blocks is verified.

68
00:04:30,057 --> 00:04:34,247
The score and successful-action count stay
equal; only presentation strength changes.

69
00:04:34,517 --> 00:04:38,247
Distinguish an individual success, a sequence,
and the completion of a longer section.

70
00:04:38,337 --> 00:04:42,587
This aligns the scale of the recognition
with the actual accomplishment.

71
00:04:43,383 --> 00:04:48,783
Hi-Fi RUSH presents continuing hits and large
actions with different visual strengths.

72
00:04:49,203 --> 00:04:54,423
While score and grade remain in the corner,
the next attack continues in the center.

73
00:04:54,763 --> 00:04:57,163
Observe whether a small success can be

74
00:04:57,163 --> 00:04:59,163
recognized without stopping for a long
sequence.

75
00:04:59,503 --> 00:05:04,383
Other combat excerpts also show brief hit
responses and stronger attack effects.

76
00:05:04,723 --> 00:05:07,983
These are not two experimental versions with
equal scoring rules;

77
00:05:08,363 --> 00:05:10,203
they help identify each display role.

78
00:05:10,203 --> 00:05:13,003
In a controlled comparison like our own,

79
00:05:13,003 --> 00:05:15,783
we can hold rules constant and change only
response strength.

80
00:05:16,423 --> 00:05:21,223
Ring Fit victory and level gains, and a Sports
rank change, mark the end of longer intervals.

81
00:05:21,763 --> 00:05:24,563
Separating movement grades from completion
celebrations

82
00:05:24,563 --> 00:05:27,163
reduces the need to exaggerate every success.

83
00:05:27,723 --> 00:05:33,103
Choose a response for one action, accumulated
performance and the end of a larger interval.

84
00:05:34,667 --> 00:05:38,990
Unconditionally calling a failure perfect
can undermine trust in the praise.

85
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:43,330
Both screens receive the same inputs,
deliberately missing one attack.

86
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:47,730
Health falls equally, but one side still
claims the performance was perfect.

87
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:52,010
The other side shows the hit and acknowledges
only the blocks that actually succeeded.

88
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:56,490
We can name a genuine partial success without hiding the failure.

89
00:05:56,720 --> 00:05:59,150
Every failure does not need a compliment.

90
00:05:59,420 --> 00:06:03,625
Show a retry clue separately, and
distinguish signals for success and failure.

91
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:08,130
Supportive wording should remain connected
to actual outcomes to inform another attempt.

92
00:06:09,067 --> 00:06:15,027
Some Hi-Fi RUSH combat excerpts show a lower
grade; observe it with the actual movement.

93
00:06:15,347 --> 00:06:21,987
Another scene shows Perfect after a movement
at a device; it grades that particular action.

94
00:06:22,247 --> 00:06:25,307
Not extending one success into a claim of
perfect

95
00:06:25,307 --> 00:06:28,387
overall play connects to honest
acknowledgement.

96
00:06:28,967 --> 00:06:32,627
An ability-state menu and a combat grade are
different;

97
00:06:33,007 --> 00:06:34,987
state information is not praise itself.

98
00:06:34,987 --> 00:06:37,727
Record the visible hit and grade without
inventing

99
00:06:37,727 --> 00:06:41,227
an unseen failure or completion condition.

100
00:06:41,667 --> 00:06:46,587
What we acknowledge during a failure should be
based on an action that actually succeeded.

101
00:06:46,827 --> 00:06:50,027
Ring Fit movement and collection display the
acquired item;

102
00:06:50,527 --> 00:06:52,047
record only the observed action.

103
00:06:52,467 --> 00:06:56,747
The following traversal alone does not prove a
perfect completion of the entire stage.

104
00:06:57,087 --> 00:06:58,327
Together with our earlier failure comparison,

105
00:06:58,327 --> 00:07:01,767
check what a truthful small acknowledgement
would be.

106
00:07:02,517 --> 00:07:06,460
Finally, check whether the praise obstructs the next action.

107
00:07:06,770 --> 00:07:11,520
Large text placed over the attack can cover
the area where the next threat must be read.

108
00:07:11,710 --> 00:07:16,180
This comparison preserves outcomes and
wording while changing position and area.

109
00:07:16,350 --> 00:07:20,180
Compare a small marker near the action
with a brief response near the edge.

110
00:07:20,590 --> 00:07:25,060
Check that neither moving targets nor necessary
information are obscured at phone size.

111
00:07:25,210 --> 00:07:29,975
Record what is recognized, when it is
recognized, and how strongly it is expressed.

112
00:07:29,990 --> 00:07:34,900
Before increasing praise frequency, check
whether the expression matches the recent action.

113
00:07:35,230 --> 00:07:39,700
Look for moments of accomplishment in
the actual actions of your own game.

114
00:07:39,883 --> 00:07:46,143
Hi-Fi RUSH combat shows moving enemies and the
character alongside corner status displays.

115
00:07:46,443 --> 00:07:51,223
Observe whether feedback can be noticed while
the next target and danger remain readable.

116
00:07:51,503 --> 00:07:54,383
Avoiding obstruction when making a response
larger

117
00:07:54,383 --> 00:07:57,423
connects to our earlier placement comparison.

118
00:07:57,743 --> 00:08:00,663
In other combat and sports scenes, examine the
center,

119
00:08:00,663 --> 00:08:03,043
corners and movement direction separately.

120
00:08:03,183 --> 00:08:08,023
Seeing a message briefly is not proof that a
person playing understood it.

121
00:08:08,023 --> 00:08:11,043
During continued movement, such as Ring Fit
traversal,

122
00:08:11,143 --> 00:08:12,983
also consider the next object to look at.

123
00:08:13,303 --> 00:08:15,923
In the final minigames, observe where scores
and

124
00:08:15,923 --> 00:08:18,063
responses such as Nice appear after real
movements.

125
00:08:18,383 --> 00:08:21,063
Turn this into a question for a person: can
they

126
00:08:21,303 --> 00:08:23,483
recognize both the action and result on a
small screen?

127
00:08:23,743 --> 00:08:27,163
Use real action scenes with timing,
specificity,

128
00:08:27,163 --> 00:08:30,103
strength, honesty and placement to design
praise.
